{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 20, :comments ["<b>it is reckoned</b> — that it is within the territory of the land of the Rephaim, meaning that it is included in it. Similarly, 'from the Shihor which is before Egypt even unto the border of Ekron northward, <b>it is reckoned</b> to the Canaanite' (Joshua 13:3), and similarly, 'the Beerothites, of the children of Benjamin, for Beeroth also <b>is reckoned</b> to Benjamin' (II Samuel 4:2). And it is possible that the phrase <b>'they too are reckoned'</b> above likewise means that the verse says regarding the Emim who dwelt in it formerly, 'they too are reckoned Rephaim' — that they were among them and were like the Anakim, and therefore they called them Emim. And this is the correct interpretation. Now the land of the Rephaim was very large: from it Moab took territory, and from it the children of Ammon took territory, and there remained of it for the Rephaim themselves, for Og who dwelt in Ashtaroth was from among them, as it says, 'And they smote the Rephaim in Ashteroth-karnaim' (Genesis 14:5), and in Joshua, 'if thou hast a land, then it shall be thine in the land of the Perizzite and of the Rephaim' (Joshua 17:15). Therefore the verse says regarding the border of Moab and Ammon, 'for it is reckoned to the Rephaim' — and the Moabites and Ammonites are the ones who changed their names to other names: Emim and Zamzummim."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 2, :notes {0 "The Joshua 17:15 citation refers to the phrase ובארץ הפרזי והרפאים; Ramban quotes it loosely, rendering the sense rather than the exact wording."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T02:44:28.353922842Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}